# Chapter 4 — The Sprite

Last chapter you drew straight onto the screen: a pixel here, a rectangle
there. That works, but it has a problem. If you want to draw a little hero and
then *move* him, you would have to erase every pixel and redraw them one step
over, every single time. That is a lot of bookkeeping for a thing that is
really just *one shape you keep putting in different places.*

A sprite is the fix. A sprite is a small picture you define **once**, give a
name, and then stamp wherever you like. This chapter is about making them and
placing them.

## A Motoi sprite is a cluster of cells, not a bitmap

Here is the one idea to hold onto, because it is a little different from other
consoles. On many machines a sprite is a fixed rectangle of pixels — an 8×8 or
16×16 grid, filled edge to edge. A Motoi sprite is looser and more honest than
that: it is **a named cluster of cells**, where each cell is just three numbers
— an `x`, a `y`, and a color. You list only the cells that are actually lit.

```
        a Motoi sprite = a bag of lit cells

           (0,0,8) (1,0,8) (2,0,8)         . R R R .
           (0,1,8)         (2,1,8)   -->    . R . R .
                   (1,2,8)                  . . R . .
```

The `x` and `y` are measured in the sprite's **own little frame**, starting
from its top-left. Color is one of the sixteen from Chapter 3. Empty spots are
simply cells you did not include — there is no \"blank\" to store. This is why
Motoi sprites can be any shape at all, not just squares.

Load the sprite library (and the framebuffer, since sprites end up drawn there):

```motoi
(require 'graphics/framebuffer)
(require 'graphics/sprite)
```

## Define a sprite, then stamp it

`sprite/define` takes a name and a list of `(x y color)` cells and files the
sprite away under that name. Later, `sprite/rasterize` stamps a named sprite
onto the screen at a chosen spot and tells you how many cells it drew:

```motoi
(require 'graphics/framebuffer)
(require 'graphics/sprite)
(sprite/define 'heart
  (list (list 0 0 8) (list 2 0 8)
        (list 0 1 8) (list 1 1 8) (list 2 1 8)
        (list 1 2 8)))
(display (sprite/rasterize 'heart 60 60))
(newline)
```

```
6
```

You defined a six-cell red heart, then stamped it with its corner at `(60, 60)`
on the screen. `sprite/rasterize` answered `6` — one for each cell that landed
on the screen. The **return value is the count of cells actually stamped**,
which is a small, useful truth: it tells you the sprite really went down, and
how much of it made it onto the visible screen. Stamp a sprite half off the
edge and the count drops, because the clipped cells do not paint.

The win is this: define once, stamp many. To draw the heart in three places you
call `sprite/rasterize` three times with three positions — you never redefine
the shape.

## You do not even have to name it first

Sometimes you have a one-off cluster and naming it would be ceremony. You can
hand `sprite/rasterize` a raw list of cells directly and it stamps them on the
spot:

```motoi
(require 'graphics/framebuffer)
(require 'graphics/sprite)
(display
  (sprite/rasterize
    (list (list 0 0 11) (list 1 1 11) (list 2 2 11))
    10 10))
(newline)
```

```
3
```

Three green cells, stamped as a little diagonal starting at `(10, 10)`. No
`sprite/define`, no name — just the cells and a place to put them. Use this for
throwaway shapes; use a named sprite for anything you will draw more than once.

## Petals: naming the parts of a sprite

A sprite is more than a picture — often you want to refer to a *spot* on it. A
hero has a head where a hat should sit and a hand where a sword attaches. Motoi
lets you name these anchor points when you define the sprite. They are called
**petals**, and they turn a shape into something you can build on.

```motoi
(require 'graphics/sprite)
(sprite/define 'hero
  (list (list 0 0 12) (list 0 1 12) (list 0 2 12))
  (list (list 'head 0 0)
        (list 'foot 0 2)))
(display (sprite/landmarks 'hero))
(newline)
(display (sprite/address 'hero 'foot))
(newline)
```

```
(head foot)
(0 2)
```

The third argument to `sprite/define` is a list of `(name x y)` petals.
`sprite/landmarks` lists the petals a sprite has — here, `head` and `foot`.
`sprite/address` resolves one petal to its coordinate — `foot` lives at
`(0, 2)` in the sprite's frame. When you later attach a hat to a hero or a
flame to a rocket, you ask for the petal by name instead of hard-coding a
number, and the attachment stays correct even if you redraw the sprite bigger.

## What you just proved

You now understand what a Motoi sprite really is — a named bag of lit cells,
not a fixed square — and you have made one, stamped it, stamped a nameless one,
and named anchor points on it with petals. Every program ran, and each returned
a real number or list you could check.

But a stamped sprite still just sits there. It does not move, because nothing
has told it to change over time. That missing piece — *time itself* — is the
next chapter, and it is the one that turns pictures into a game.

> **Tip**
>
> Because a sprite is only its lit cells, a big mostly-empty shape costs almost
> nothing to store. Do not pad your sprites with blank cells to make them
> rectangular — leave the gaps out. Motoi is happiest drawing exactly the dots
> you mean.

> **Warning**
>
> `sprite/rasterize` returns how many cells it stamped. If it answers `0`, the
> sprite did not draw — usually because the name is misspelled (an unknown
> sprite stamps nothing) or the whole shape landed off-screen. When a sprite
> vanishes, check that count first; it is the console telling you the stamp
> found nothing to do.

